WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.813
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Foundations of Big
Data Exploration as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.813 --> 00:00:10.031
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:10.031 --> 00:00:15.476
NARRATOR: This course provides a broad
introduction to the exploration and management

4
00:00:15.476 --> 00:00:20.468
of large datasets being generated and used in
the modern world.

5
00:00:20.468 --> 00:00:27.188
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Urban
crime datasets require systematic exploration

6
00:00:27.188 --> 00:00:32.687
to identify patterns in location, frequency,
and temporal trends.

7
00:00:32.687 --> 00:00:39.905
NARRATOR: On screen, display a map overlay
showing urban crime hotspots with a legend

8
00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:42.999
explaining data density and temporal filters.

9
00:00:42.999 --> 00:00:49.081
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

10
00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:50.484
change the conclusion.

11
00:00:50.484 --> 00:00:56.124
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

12
00:00:56.234 --> 00:01:02.025
NARRATOR: Now define Data Preparation and
Workflow Structure precisely enough that

13
00:01:02.025 --> 00:01:05.184
another learner could test the definition.

14
00:01:05.184 --> 00:01:11.817
NARRATOR: Data-analysis workflows separate
data preparation, exploratory analysis,

15
00:01:11.817 --> 00:01:17.621
training, validation, testing, model
selection, and evaluation.

16
00:01:17.621 --> 00:01:25.162
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Cleaning
census data involves handling missing values

17
00:01:25.162 --> 00:01:28.933
and standardizing categorical variables before
analysis.

18
00:01:28.934 --> 00:01:36.943
NARRATOR: On screen, show a linear workflow
diagram with nodes for preparation,

19
00:01:36.943 --> 00:01:42.949
exploration, training, validation, and
evaluation, connected by directional arrows.

20
00:01:42.949 --> 00:01:49.512
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

21
00:01:49.512 --> 00:01:51.699
evidence would distinguish them.

22
00:01:51.699 --> 00:01:57.996
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

23
00:01:57.996 --> 00:02:05.178
NARRATOR: Name each dependency explicitly, and
keep labels and representations consistent

24
00:02:05.178 --> 00:02:07.137
throughout the analysis.

25
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:13.073
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Supervised and Unsupervised

26
00:02:13.073 --> 00:02:16.778
Learning Methods operates in a concrete
sequence.

27
00:02:16.778 --> 00:02:24.340
NARRATOR: Supervised methods learn mappings
from examples paired with target labels, while

28
00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:28.121
unsupervised methods identify structure
without targets.

29
00:02:28.121 --> 00:02:34.657
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case:
Predicting drug response requires supervised

30
00:02:34.657 --> 00:02:41.846
learning with patient outcomes as labels,
whereas clustering patient demographics uses

31
00:02:41.846 --> 00:02:43.153
unsupervised methods.

32
00:02:43.153 --> 00:02:50.859
NARRATOR: On screen, split a diagram into two
columns: left shows labeled examples mapping

33
00:02:50.859 --> 00:02:55.262
to outcomes, right shows unlabeled data
forming clusters.

34
00:02:55.262 --> 00:03:02.403
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

35
00:03:02.403 --> 00:03:07.996
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

36
00:03:08.114 --> 00:03:14.226
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Preprocessing and Data Leakage Prevention

37
00:03:14.226 --> 00:03:17.282
and make the decision criteria explicit.

38
00:03:17.282 --> 00:03:23.782
NARRATOR: Preprocessing must be fitted only on
permitted training data to avoid leakage.

39
00:03:23.782 --> 00:03:31.201
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Scaling
features using only the training set mean and

40
00:03:31.201 --> 00:03:36.501
standard deviation prevents test set
information from influencing model parameters.

41
00:03:36.501 --> 00:03:44.052
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a pipeline
where a scaler transforms training data, then

42
00:03:44.052 --> 00:03:49.860
applies the same transformation to validation
data without recalculating parameters.

43
00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:56.635
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

44
00:03:56.635 --> 00:03:58.329
move the goalposts.

45
00:03:58.329 --> 00:04:05.345
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

46
00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:11.645
NARRATOR: Apply Pipelines and Consistent
Transformations to a worked situation instead

47
00:04:11.645 --> 00:04:15.587
of treating it as an isolated definition.

48
00:04:15.587 --> 00:04:22.779
NARRATOR: Pipelines combine transformations
and estimators so the same operations are

49
00:04:22.779 --> 00:04:24.087
applied consistently.

50
00:04:24.087 --> 00:04:31.142
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A machine
learning pipeline applies standardization,

51
00:04:31.142 --> 00:04:36.915
imputation, and feature selection sequentially
to ensure reproducible results.

52
00:04:36.915 --> 00:04:43.576
NARRATOR: On screen, render a horizontal
pipeline graphic with icons for each step,

53
00:04:43.576 --> 00:04:49.212
showing data flowing through and emerging as a
final model output.

54
00:04:49.212 --> 00:04:55.993
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

55
00:04:55.993 --> 00:04:57.446
at each step.

56
00:04:57.446 --> 00:05:02.728
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

57
00:05:02.834 --> 00:05:07.861
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in
Cross-Validation and Model Evaluation,

58
00:05:07.861 --> 00:05:12.330
including what improves and what becomes more
difficult.

59
00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:19.190
NARRATOR: Cross-validation repeats training
and validation across defined splits.

60
00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:25.752
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: K-fold
cross-validation evaluates a classification

61
00:05:25.752 --> 00:05:33.627
model for social networking fraud detection by
rotating validation sets across training

62
00:05:33.627 --> 00:05:34.283
folds.

63
00:05:34.284 --> 00:05:41.052
NARRATOR: On screen, draw a circular diagram
with five segments labeled as folds,

64
00:05:41.052 --> 00:05:48.341
highlighting one fold as validation while
others are training, with a metric box showing

65
00:05:48.341 --> 00:05:48.862
accuracy.

66
00:05:48.862 --> 00:05:55.453
NARRATOR: State the benefit, the cost, and the
condition under which the tradeoff is

67
00:05:55.453 --> 00:05:55.924
acceptable.

68
00:05:55.924 --> 00:06:03.221
NARRATOR: Making the tradeoff visible prevents
a local improvement from hiding a larger cost.

69
00:06:03.338 --> 00:06:08.805
NARRATOR: Stress-test the reasoning around
Traditional Database Systems and Query

70
00:06:08.805 --> 00:06:15.366
Languages with an edge case and identify which
assumption controls the outcome.

71
00:06:15.366 --> 00:06:23.736
NARRATOR: Data systems organize records with
declared types, tables, rows, columns, keys,

72
00:06:23.736 --> 00:06:25.131
and constraints.

73
00:06:25.131 --> 00:06:31.596
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
hospital database stores patient records in

74
00:06:31.596 --> 00:06:38.600
tables with defined data types, primary keys,
and foreign key constraints to maintain

75
00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:39.678
referential integrity.

76
00:06:39.678 --> 00:06:46.710
NARRATOR: On screen, show a relational schema
diagram with tables, columns, data types,

77
00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:51.037
primary keys, and foreign key relationships
between them.

78
00:06:51.037 --> 00:06:57.155
NARRATOR: Change one assumption at a time and
observe which part of the reasoning no longer

79
00:06:57.155 --> 00:06:57.537
holds.

80
00:06:57.537 --> 00:07:03.744
NARRATOR: The edge case reveals the boundary
of the claim without discarding the useful

81
00:07:03.744 --> 00:07:04.631
core idea.

82
00:07:04.754 --> 00:07:12.302
NARRATOR: Use Query Operations and Indexing to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

83
00:07:12.302 --> 00:07:14.625
causes, and corrective action.

84
00:07:14.625 --> 00:07:21.110
NARRATOR: Queries select, join, aggregate,
order, and transform data.

85
00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:27.453
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A query
joins patient records with medication logs,

86
00:07:27.453 --> 00:07:34.771
aggregates daily prescriptions, orders by
date, and uses an index on the date column to

87
00:07:34.771 --> 00:07:36.235
speed up retrieval.

88
00:07:36.235 --> 00:07:43.907
NARRATOR: On screen, display a SQL query
structure with highlighted SELECT, JOIN, GROUP

89
00:07:43.907 --> 00:07:51.578
BY, ORDER BY clauses, alongside an index tree
diagram showing optimized data access.

90
00:07:51.578 --> 00:07:57.241
NARRATOR: Locate the first point where
observed behavior diverges from expected

91
00:07:57.241 --> 00:08:00.844
behavior, then test the smallest plausible
cause.

92
00:08:00.844 --> 00:08:06.994
NARRATOR: The diagnosis is strongest when
another person can reproduce the observation

93
00:08:06.994 --> 00:08:08.532
and the test.

94
00:08:08.642 --> 00:08:14.642
NARRATOR: Connect Data Quality and Integrity
Management to the earlier ideas and explain

95
00:08:14.642 --> 00:08:17.873
how the pieces work as one system.

96
00:08:17.873 --> 00:08:24.357
NARRATOR: Data quality analysis makes
missingness, duplicates, inconsistent

97
00:08:24.357 --> 00:08:30.842
representation, integrity constraints,
sampling, and update behavior explicit.

98
00:08:30.842 --> 00:08:38.342
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A census
dataset requires explicit handling of

99
00:08:38.342 --> 00:08:44.591
duplicate household records, inconsistent
address formats, and missing population counts

100
00:08:44.591 --> 00:08:45.841
before analysis.

101
00:08:45.841 --> 00:08:53.059
NARRATOR: On screen, present a checklist
graphic listing missingness, duplicates,

102
00:08:53.059 --> 00:08:59.554
inconsistent representation, integrity
constraints, sampling, and update behavior as

103
00:08:59.554 --> 00:09:00.998
quality metrics.

104
00:09:00.998 --> 00:09:09.748
NARRATOR: Explain each connection explicitly
and verify that information retains the same

105
00:09:09.748 --> 00:09:11.935
meaning across representations.

106
00:09:11.935 --> 00:09:18.566
NARRATOR: The synthesis matters only if every
connection remains supported by the original

107
00:09:18.566 --> 00:09:19.076
evidence.

108
00:09:19.202 --> 00:09:24.813
NARRATOR: Finish with Practical Analysis
Checklist as a practical checklist that can

109
00:09:24.813 --> 00:09:26.683
guide a new analysis.

110
00:09:26.683 --> 00:09:33.824
NARRATOR: Analytical work should preserve
provenance from captured data through

111
00:09:33.824 --> 00:09:38.823
cleaning, transformation, grouping, modeling,
visualization, and reporting.

112
00:09:38.823 --> 00:09:47.073
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A space
exploration project maintains a traceable log

113
00:09:47.073 --> 00:09:52.785
from raw satellite telemetry through cleaning,
transformation, grouping, modeling,

114
00:09:52.785 --> 00:09:55.323
visualization, and final reporting.

115
00:09:55.323 --> 00:10:03.227
NARRATOR: On screen, show a circular
provenance loop with arrows connecting each

116
00:10:03.227 --> 00:10:07.837
stage: captured data, cleaning,
transformation, grouping, modeling,

117
00:10:07.837 --> 00:10:12.448
visualization, reporting, and back to captured
data.

118
00:10:12.448 --> 00:10:20.091
NARRATOR: For each checklist item, record the
evidence, the remaining uncertainty, and the

119
00:10:20.091 --> 00:10:21.855
next verification step.

120
00:10:21.855 --> 00:10:27.631
NARRATOR: The result is a repeatable
procedure, not a conclusion that depends on

121
00:10:27.631 --> 00:10:28.964
memory or intuition.
